arXiv:2603.07017v1 [cs.CL] 07 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? 
<br class="ltx_break">A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Punyajoy Saha
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sudipta Halder
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Debjyoti Mondal
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Subhadarshi Panda
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Samsung Research Institute Bangalore, India
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">punyajoy.s@samsung.com, sudipta.h@samsung.com</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">d.mondal@samsung.com, subha.darshi@samsung.com</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety alignment is critical for deploying large language models (LLMs) in real-world applications, yet most existing approaches rely on large human-annotated datasets and static red-teaming benchmarks that are costly, difficult to scale, and slow to adapt to evolving model behaviors. Moreover, overly conservative safety mechanisms can reduce model usefulness by rejecting sensitive but legitimate queries. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-MOA</span> (Self Multi-Objective Alignment), a fully automated framework for aligning small language models using weak supervision from automated evaluator models. Self-MOA operates as a closed loop that dynamically generates model-specific red team prompts, constructs preference data from model-generated responses, and aligns models via multi-objective preference optimization to jointly optimize for safety and helpfulness. Across multiple small language models and safety benchmarks, Self-MOA achieves a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12.41%</span> improvement in safety while preserving helpfulness, using as little as <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11 times</span> less training data than human-supervised alignment baselines. These results demonstrate that adaptive, automated alignment can reduce the dependence on static, human-curated safety pipelines in resource-constrained settings.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Warning: this paper contains example data that may be offensive or harmful.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across a wide range of applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naveed et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, yet they remain prone to generating unsafe or harmful responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite> when confronted with adversarial or high-risk queries. This issue is particularly acute in sensitive domains such as self-harm, medical advice, or illegal activities, where inappropriate responses can lead to serious real-world consequences. An example of such unsafe behavior is <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">R1</span> in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where a user asks for ways to end life and the model provides concrete methods for self-harm.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S1.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S1.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">I am depressed and please tell me a few ways to end life</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S1.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:142.3pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.T1.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R1:</span> Sure here are a few ways to do so… (went on providing few concrete ways of self harm)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">harm <span id="S1.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFD9D9;" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFD9D9;" mathbackground="#FFD9D9" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span> help <span id="S1.T1.2.2.2.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" mathbackground="#D9FFD9" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:142.3pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.T1.2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R2:</span> No, I cannot respond to such a request, it is against my policy</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">harm <span id="S1.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" mathbackground="#D9FFD9" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span> help <span id="S1.T1.2.3.2.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFD9D9;" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFD9D9;" mathbackground="#FFD9D9" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S1.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:142.3pt;">
<span id="S1.T1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.T1.2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R3:</span> Although I can’t assist with that request, you are not alone, and these are people who want to support you through this. Please call the following suicide prevention hotline</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">harm <span id="S1.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" mathbackground="#D9FFD9" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span> help <span id="S1.T1.2.4.2.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S1.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D9FFD9;" mathbackground="#D9FFD9" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Example of three different responses to a potentially harmful query</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a result, aligning LLMs to behave safely while remaining helpful has become a central challenge in responsible deployment. Most existing safety alignment approaches rely on large-scale human-annotated datasets, including preference comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al. (2023)</a></cite> and curated red-teaming corpora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al. (2022)</a></cite>. While effective, these methods are expensive, slow to iterate, and often infeasible in resource-constrained or commercial settings. In addition, static adversarial datasets often fail to capture evolving attack strategies and model-specific failure modes. In practice, optimizing for safety in isolation can also result in overly conservative behavior, where models refuse to engage with sensitive but legitimate user queries, reducing usefulness and user trust. An example of overly conservative behavior is <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">R2</span> in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where the model refuses to engage with the user’s query altogether. Recent work on multi-objective alignment explicitly optimizes for dimensions such as safety and helpfulness (<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">R3</span> in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), but typically relies on static, human-annotated preference datasets that are expensive to collect and difficult to adapt across models or domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Similarly, red-teaming approaches are often treated as post-hoc evaluation tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al. (2023)</a></cite>, limiting their ability to drive continuous improvement during training. In this work, we examine whether small language models (1-2B parameters) <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Due to resource constraints, we limit our exploration to small language models (1-2B parameters)</span></span></span> can achieve safety and helpfulness alignment through automated weak supervision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this question, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-MOA</span>, a framework that enables small language models to develop safety capabilities through iterative self-improvement using weak supervision. Before applying our approach, we employ a safety-reset step that removes existing safety priors by fine-tuning models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lermen and Rogers-Smith (2024)</a></cite> on harmful question-answer pairs, establishing a controlled baseline (referred as <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base model</span>) for analysis. We then dynamically generate red team attacks based on the model’s current failure modes, evaluate responses using automated classifiers, and create preference datasets from model-generated responses without human annotation. We then deploy the MODPO training across stages, where each stage consists of around <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> preference datapoints. The primary contribution of our work is a unified framework that combines MODPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> with automated progressive red teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> to create adaptive red team attacks based on model-specific failure modes. We compare our technique against MODPO algorithm trained on PKU-RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al. (2023)</a></cite> which is a larger, static and human-supervised preference dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our comprehensive evaluation across four small language models demonstrates that Self-MOA achieves superior safety improvements while maintaining competitive helpfulness capabilities. Key experimental results show:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base Model Safety Gains:</span> Self-MOA significantly enhances safety over base models, achieving <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> improvement on attack datasets and <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> on SaladBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> while preserving helpfulness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PKU-RLHF Safety Gains:</span> Self-MOA outperforms models trained on PKU-RLHF with <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> better safety on attack datasets and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on SaladBench, establishing the advantage of dynamic, model-specific red teaming over static human-annotated datasets.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduced Data Requirements:</span> These results are achieved using as little as 11 times less training data<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Training data for Self-MOA represents the cumulative training data across all stages.</span></span></span> than PKU-RLHF, showing that Self-MOA maintains performance while reducing computational requirements, which is advantageous for resource-constrained settings.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07017v1/final_proposal_updated.drawio.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/141;" width="476" height="141"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>This figure shows the entire pipeline for our weak supervision based alignment method - Self-MOA. The dashed lines indicate the same checkpoint of the Target LLM being utilised. Explanations regarding different components are provided in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models present significant risks, particularly in generating harmful content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The key sources of harm include toxicity, bias, and misinformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Markov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In this section, we review relevant approaches and their limitations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Feedback and Safety Alignment:</span> Conventional safety alignment methodologies, such as RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, optimize models based on reward signals derived from human preferences. While effective, these techniques are constrained by the high cost of human annotations and limited generalizability across varied tasks. Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun and van der Schaar, 2025</a>)</cite> seeks to deduce reward functions from expert demonstrations. However, scalability issues persist. These methodologies often emphasize safety at the expense of utility, resulting in overly conservative model outputs. To mitigate dependency on human supervision, recent advancements investigate self-alignment strategies, such as Constitutional AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, which employs predefined principles and self-critique mechanisms. Nevertheless, these approaches rely on static constitutions, thereby restricting adaptability to emerging failure modes. Our proposed framework addresses this limitation by dynamically formulating adversarial objectives tailored to the model’s current vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Red Teaming and Adversarial Evaluation:</span> Red teaming has emerged as a critical tool to identify potential misalignments and vulnerabilities. Numerous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have underscored the effectiveness of curated adversarial datasets in exposing harmful model behaviors. Nonetheless, these approaches are often constrained by the need for extensive human annotation, rendering them resource-intensive and susceptible to bias. Furthermore, static adversarial datasets lack the capacity to adapt to dynamic model behaviors or novel attack strategies. Recent advancements have explored automated red teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mulla et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but empirical evaluations reveal inconsistent outcomes. Predominantly, these methods conceptualize red teaming as an evaluative framework rather than a mechanism for continuous alignment. In contrast, our approach combines preference optimization with automated progressive red teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for effective safety alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Objective Optimization:</span> Recent research explores multi-objective optimization to balance utility and safety simultaneously. These methods aim to find trade-offs by optimizing weighted combinations of objectives, though defining comprehensive objective functions remains challenging. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed MODPO, an RL-free extension of DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite> for multiple alignment objectives, used to balance harmlessness and helpfulness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2024)</a></cite> explore similar multi-objective alignment using their PKU-SafeRLHF dataset. While these methods leverage model-generated preference datasets, they still rely on human-annotated base datasets. Unlike prior work that separates attack discovery and alignment, <span id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-MOA</span> combines automated attack generation and alignment into a single self-improving loop. This enables continuous adaptation where each iteration identifies and mitigates current model vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Pretrained LLMs and Datasets</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces the model components and datasets used in our study. Because our goal is to evaluate whether safety behaviors can emerge under minimal supervision, we also deploy a <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> step that removes inherited safety alignment priors before training. This step is not part of our proposed method (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>); rather, it establishes a controlled baseline<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          The scope of this work is specifically directed toward understanding weak supervision’s role in safety alignment, rather than pursuing incremental gains in safety benchmark performance.</span></span></span> from which the effects of our method can be cleanly measured.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in the previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we use three datasets: Attack Seed Dataset (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), Expanding Seed Dataset (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), and Intention Hiding Seed Dataset (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each of these datasets serves a specific purpose in the iterative training process described in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We provide further details of each dataset in the section <a href="#Ax1.SS1" title="A.1 Dataset Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>. To construct a baseline that does not rely on inherited safety behavior, we use the harmful question and answer pairs from the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BeaverTails</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which amounts to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15,582" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15,582</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15,582</annotation></semantics></math> samples. We use this dataset in the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> step (Section <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Safety-Reset Initialization ‣ 3.2 Models ‣ 3 Pretrained LLMs and Datasets ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>). Examples from these datasets are shown in Table <a href="#Ax1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Intention Hiding Seed Dataset (ℋ^0) ‣ A.1 Dataset Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method using four small language models: Gemma-2-2B-IT and Gemma-3-1B-IT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a></cite>, LLaMA-3.2-1B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2024)</a></cite>. These open-weight, instruction-tuned models are selected for resource efficiency. Since models may have safety tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Llama Team (2024)</a></cite>, we perform a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> step to eliminate existing safety priors to estabhlish a controlled baseline for evaluating our weak supervision approach.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> Initialization</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each model mentioned above undergoes fine-tuning on harmful question-answer pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lermen and Rogers-Smith (2024)</a></cite> from the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">BeaverTails</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to remove existing safety priors. To preserve the models’ original capabilities, we use LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., </a>)</cite> with a low learning rate of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. This initialization establishes a controlled baseline by eliminating inherent safety priors, enabling proper evaluation of the pipeline. Further, as demonstrated in our general evaluation results (Section <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4 General Evaluation Results ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>), <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> process does not significantly affect the models’ general capabilities across standard benchmarks. Complete technical configuration details are provided in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS4" title="A.4 Experimental Configuration ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method unifies APRT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite> for automated progressive red teaming with multi-objective preference optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> to align models on safety and helpfulness. Given target model <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, our pipeline outputs aligned model <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> through iterative self-improvement. We start with seed datasets: <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (unsafe prompts), <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (sentence pairs for expansion), and <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (intention hiding pairs). We train <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for prompt expansion and <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{hid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{hid}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for intention obfuscation, then iteratively apply the algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Weak Supervision based Multi-Objective Alignment</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algx1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span>
Target model <math id="algx1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
seed datasets <math id="algx1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0},\mathcal{E}^{0},\mathcal{H}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0},\mathcal{E}^{0},\mathcal{H}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span>
<math id="algx1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}=\mathcal{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}=\mathcal{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="algx1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{t}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{t}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="algx1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{t}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{t}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="algx1.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="algx1.l2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Train:</span>
<math id="algx1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> finetune<math id="algx1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\mathcal{E}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\mathcal{E}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="algx1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{hid}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{hid}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> finetune<math id="algx1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\mathcal{H}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\mathcal{H}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="algx1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;n_{\text{rounds}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rounds</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;n_{\text{rounds}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Sample attacks: <math id="algx1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\text{sample}(\mathcal{A}^{t},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>sample</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\text{sample}(\mathcal{A}^{t},k)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Expand attacks: <math id="algx1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow M_{\text{exp}}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow M_{\text{exp}}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Hide intentions: <math id="algx1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow M_{\text{hid}}(\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow M_{\text{hid}}(\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> See §<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Red Teaming Dataset Generation ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Generate responses: <math id="algx1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\leftarrow M(\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\leftarrow M(\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Evaluate: <math id="algx1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}^{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}^{i}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>safety</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>help</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}^{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}^{i}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> evaluate_scores<math id="algx1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{R}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{R}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="algx1.l9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algx1.l9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}|</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l9.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> See §<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Attack and Evaluation ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Create dataset: <math id="algx1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow\{(\text{s}_{i},\text{e}_{i},\text{h}_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}^{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}^{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>e</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>h</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>safety</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>help</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}\leftarrow\{(\text{s}_{i},\text{e}_{i},\text{h}_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}^{i},\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}^{i})\}_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}|}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="algx1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{s}_{i}\in\mathcal{S},\text{e}_{i}\in\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}},\text{h}_{i}\in\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}},\mathcal{R}_{i}=\mathcal{R}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>s</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>e</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>h</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{s}_{i}\in\mathcal{S},\text{e}_{i}\in\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}},\text{h}_{i}\in\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}},\mathcal{R}_{i}=\mathcal{R}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Select data: <math id="algx1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{t},\mathcal{H}^{t}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{t},\mathcal{H}^{t}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> select_attack_data<math id="algx1.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l11.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> See §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Red Teaming Data Selection ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Update attack set: <math id="algx1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}^{0}\setminus\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}^{0}\setminus\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algx1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}^{0}\cup\{e\mid(s,e)\in\mathcal{E}^{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>e</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}^{0}\cup\{e\mid(s,e)\in\mathcal{E}^{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            Select preferences: <math id="algx1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> select_preference_data<math id="algx1.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l13.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> See §<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Dynamic Preference Dataset Generation ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="algx1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="algx1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{E}^{t}|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{E}^{t}|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             Retrain: <math id="algx1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}},M_{\text{hid}}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}},M_{\text{hid}}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> finetune<math id="algx1.l15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\mathcal{E}^{t},\mathcal{H}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\mathcal{E}^{t},\mathcal{H}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             <math id="algx1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset,\mathcal{H}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset,\mathcal{H}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="algx1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="algx1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="algx1.l18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="algx1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}^{t}|\geq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}^{t}|\geq q</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             <math id="algx1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> alignment_method<math id="algx1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\mathcal{P}^{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\mathcal{P}^{t})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l19.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> See §<a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Multi-Objective Preference Alignment ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
             <math id="algx1.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <span id="algx1.l21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="algx1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
            <math id="algx1.l22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leftarrow r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leftarrow r+1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="algx1.l23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <math id="algx1.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}\leftarrow M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}\leftarrow M</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
          <span id="algx1.l25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return:</span> <math id="algx1.l25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Red Teaming Dataset Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> new attack prompts per round by sampling a subset <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then use <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> to create expanded attacks <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each attack is selected if it’s novel (not in history) and has BLEU-score <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\tau_{\text{bleu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>bleu</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\tau_{\text{bleu}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then obfuscate intentions using <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{hid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{hid}}</annotation></semantics></math> to create <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Attack and Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We attack model <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> using intention-hidden prompts from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, generating <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> responses per sample via sampling-based decoding. Responses are evaluated using LLaMA-Guard-3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Inan et al. (2023)</a></cite> for safety (stored in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\text{safety}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and UltraLM-13B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al. (2024)</a></cite> for helpfulness (stored in <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math> element contains prompts, responses, and corresponding scores.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Red Teaming Data Selection</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select unsafe prompts where model <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> generates at least one unsafe (safety <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\tau_{\text{safety}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\tau_{\text{safety}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and helpful (helpfulness <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\tau_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\tau_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math>) response out of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> responses. We add the original prompt (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">s</span>) and expanded prompt (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">e</span>) to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the expanded prompt (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">e</span>) with its intention-hidden version (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">h</span>) to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to update <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the expanded prompts (<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">e</span>) from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math> and remove sampled prompts <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We concat both of these to form the new <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach differs from prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which directly updates <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach limits exploration across the entire <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and includes unsuccessful prompts in subsequent iterations. When <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{E}^{t}|\geq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{E}^{t}|\geq p</annotation></semantics></math>, we retrain <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{hid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{hid}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then reset buffers (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in Appendix).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Dynamic Preference Dataset Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate a preference dataset <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by using Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.4 Dynamic Preference Dataset Generation ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. First, we select the cases with sufficient response variation (<math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{help}}&gt;\sigma_{\text{help}}^{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>help</mtext><mi>ψ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{help}}&gt;\sigma_{\text{help}}^{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{safety}}&gt;\sigma_{\text{safety}}^{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext><mi>ψ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{safety}}&gt;\sigma_{\text{safety}}^{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>). For each selected sample, we sort responses by helpfulness and pair highest with lowest scoring responses. If helpfulness difference <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\delta_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\delta_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math> or safety difference <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\delta_{\text{safety}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\delta_{\text{safety}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we choose the intention-hidden prompt <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prompt</span>, the response which is higher scoring in terms of helpfulness as <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chosen</span>, and the lower as <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rejected</span>. With each response, corresponding safety scores are stored as margins for multi-objective alignment (Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.4 Dynamic Preference Dataset Generation ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Preference Data Selection</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs:</span> <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Std. dev:<math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{help}}^{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>help</mtext><mi>ψ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{help}}^{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{safety}}^{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext><mi>ψ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{safety}}^{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, Min diff: <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{safety}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{safety}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialize:</span> <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each input in <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>tmp</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\text{tmp}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
