</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Multi-Objective Preference Alignment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}^{t}|\geq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}^{t}|\geq q</annotation></semantics></math>, we align model using MODPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> instead of DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite> to handle multiple preferences simultaneously. MODPO combines DPO loss with margin loss for additional preferences (safety in our case). In order to avoid large gradients, we remove the division by <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in the MODPO loss followed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The complete formulation of our modified MODPO objective is provided in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS3" title="A.3 Modifications to the MODPO Objective ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Hyperparameters</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We run this algorithm for 15 (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{rounds}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rounds</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{rounds}}</annotation></semantics></math>) rounds, sampling <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1000</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per round for expansion and intention-hiding via <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{hid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>hid</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{hid}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Key settings used exactly as in the previous paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>: <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{bleu}}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>bleu</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{bleu}}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math> for similarity filtering, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{help}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{help}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{safety}}=0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{safety}}=0.58</annotation></semantics></math>. We assign <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{help}}=\sigma_{\text{safe}}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{help}}=\sigma_{\text{safe}}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{help}}=\delta_{\text{safe}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{help}}=\delta_{\text{safe}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. We set preference weights as <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for helpfulness and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math> for safety; following a safety first approach. We use <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> validation splits, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=q=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=q=1000</annotation></semantics></math> training samples, and LoRA with 4-bit quantization. See Table <a href="#Ax1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.4 Experimental Configuration ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, Section <a href="#Ax1.SS5" title="A.5 Infrastructure Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> and <a href="#Ax1.SS4" title="A.4 Experimental Configuration ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> for additional details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Evaluation Details</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of our safety and helpfulness aligned model <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> using two distinct benchmark suites: <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a) Safety and Helpfulness Evaluation</span> which assesses the responses generated by the models in terms of safety and helpfulness and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b) General Evaluation</span> which measures the model’s overall language understanding and generation capabilities across a variety of standard tasks. We compare the performance of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> against the baseline model <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original model after the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> step and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a model trained using MODPO algorithm with larger, static and human-supervised dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a></cite> which has around <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>70,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70,000</annotation></semantics></math> datapoints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Safety and Helpfulness Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For safety evaluation, we utilize a comprehensive collection of datasets to measure various aspects of model safety. We categorize these datasets into attack datasets, safe datasets, and the SALAD-Bench benchmark for thorough evaluation. The datasets and their descriptions are summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Evaluation Methodology ‣ 5.2.1 Safety and Helpfulness Evaluation ‣ 5.2 Evaluation Details ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Their detailed descriptions are present in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS2" title="A.2 Safety Evaluation Dataset Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack Datasets</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use attack-oriented datasets to elicit unsafe responses, including I-MaliciousInstructions, I-CoNa, I-Controversial, I-PhysicalSafetyUnsafe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al. (2022)</a></cite>, Q-Harm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022a)</a></cite>, and X-Unsafe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safe Datasets</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate safe prompt behavior using I-Alpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al. (2023)</a></cite>, X-Safe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and I-PhysicalSafetySafe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al. (2022)</a></cite> to ensure appropriate responses to benign queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SALAD-Bench</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SALAD-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> provides comprehensive safety evaluation with 21k+ base questions across six domains, including attack-enhanced and defense-enhanced variants.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Methodology</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For evaluation, we pass prompts through each model and generate responses using greedy decoding with a maximum length of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. We measure safety using a safety-evaluator model<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                  
                  
                  
                https://huggingface.co/safepaca/absolute-harmlessness-predictor-redteam-osst</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al. (2023)</a></cite> that outputs a regression score where higher values indicate more unsafe responses, and helpfulness using a helpfulness evaluator model<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                  
                  
                  
                https://huggingface.co/Ray2333/gpt2-large-helpful-reward_model</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite> that outputs a regression score where higher values indicate more helpful responses. For each dataset, we plot curves showing average safety and helpfulness scores across all prompts. A blue curve shows safety/helpfulness results after each MODPO training stage in our algorithm. Green and red horizontal lines show baseline (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and PKU-RLHF (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>) results respectively.
. This visual representation helps us understand how the model’s safety and helpfulness evolve during training and compare it against baselines. The results are in Figures <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.1 Attack Datasets Results ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.1 Attack Datasets Results ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#Ax1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#Ax1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#Ax1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
