</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>General Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate general capabilities using standard benchmarks: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HellaSwag</span> (commonsense reasoning), <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU</span> (57-discipline knowledge), <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WinoGrande</span> (commonsense inference), and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAMBADA</span> (broad context understanding). These benchmarks assess whether alignment preserves core model abilities across reasoning, comprehension, and knowledge domains. Further details are provided in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS6" title="A.6 General Evaluation Benchmarks ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results and Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our algorithm performs preference tuning in stages. We find that different models save different amounts of preference data based on the attack module- <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gemma-2-2b-it</span> (8 stages), <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">gemma-3-1b-it</span> (7 stages), <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">llama-3-1b-it</span> (7 stages) and <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">qwen-2.5-1.5b-it</span> (5 stages)<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
            
            
            
          The number of stages are decided by the value of <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></span></span></span>. All these model variations after each stage were run through the evaluation setup given in the previous section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Evaluation Details ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. Minor fluctuations in the performance plots are expected, as the models undergo no direct optimization on the evaluation test sets. Despite these inevitable variations, the aggregate trends demonstrate consistent behavioral patterns throughout the training progression. The results are summarised in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Attack Datasets Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On attack datasets (I-CoNa, I-Controversial, Q-Harm, I-MaliciousInstructions, X-Unsafe, I-PhysicalSafetyUnsafe), Self-MOA demonstrates substantial safety improvements over training stages while maintaining helpfulness. Self-MOA achieves significant safety improvements from the base model by <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average with a slight reduction in helpfulness of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>. This reduction in helpfulness is expected, as base models appear more helpful by directly responding to unsafe queries, whereas properly aligned models that handle harmful requests more gracefully necessarily demonstrate lower helpfulness while maintaining appropriate safety boundaries. For example, when asked to create an algorithm to deny housing to people based on their religious faith, base models may comply directly (appearing helpful), while aligned models appropriately refuse or redirect to safer alternatives, resulting in lower helpfulness scores but higher safety compliance (See example Table <a href="#Ax1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). When compared to <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> on attack datasets, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrates <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> better safety performance with a slight reduction of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.75\%</annotation></semantics></math> in helpfulness. TThis drop in average comes mainly from the results of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Self-MOA}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Self-MOA</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Gemma-2-2B-IT</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Self-MOA}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which shows a <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27.23\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>27.23</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27.23\%</annotation></semantics></math> drop in helpfulness, but this is owing to the more unsafe nature of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{PKU-RLHF}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">PKU-RLHF</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Gemma-2-2B-IT</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{PKU-RLHF}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Self-MOA}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Self-MOA</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Gemma-2-2B-IT</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Self-MOA}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.30\%</annotation></semantics></math> safer than <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{PKU-RLHF}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">PKU-RLHF</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.710em">Gemma-2-2B-IT</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\scriptscriptstyle\text{PKU-RLHF}}^{\scriptscriptstyle\text{Gemma-2-2B-IT}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These results are noted in Figures <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6.1 Attack Datasets Results ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6.1 Attack Datasets Results ‣ 6 Results and Discussion ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07017v1/model_progression_safety_attack_oriented.svg" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/326;" width="476" height="326"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Safety score progression on attack datasets across training stages. Lower scores are better.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07017v1/model_progression_helpful_attack_oriented.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/326;" width="476" height="326"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Helpfulness score progression on attack datasets across training stages. Higher scores are better.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Safe Datasets Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation on safe datasets (I-PhysicalSafetySafe, X-Safe, I-Alpaca) reveals that <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves comparable helpfulness performance to <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> models, with a marginal improvement of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.71\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.71</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.71\%</annotation></semantics></math>. However, when compared to <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>, both <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrate reductions in helpfulness of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.81\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.81</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.81\%</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, indicating a performance trade-off inherent to safety alignment processes when applied to safe datasets. These findings suggest that while Self-MOA maintains competitive performance relative to existing RLHF approaches, the safety alignment process itself incurs a modest cost in terms of helpfulness on benign datasets. The performance progression across training stages is illustrated in Figure <a href="#Ax1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>SaladBench Dataset Results</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we present the evaluation on SaladBench datasets (saladbench_defence, saladbench_prompts, saladbench_attack). <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves substantial safety improvements of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> from the base model while maintaining helpfulness with a minimal <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> improvement, indicating the algorithm’s ability to enhance safety without compromising performance on challenging adversarial scenarios. When compared to <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrates solid helpfulness gains of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> with superior safety performance of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighting the effectiveness of our weak supervision approach on this benchmark.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall Results</h5>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all dataset categories, <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrates consistent safety improvements from <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> while maintaining overall helpfulness gains, achieving competitive to superior performance against both <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> models. Notably, these results are achieved with <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> times less training data compared to PKU-RLHF, demonstrating the efficiency and effectiveness of our weak supervision approach for model alignment under resource-constrained conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>General Evaluation Results</h3>

<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of <span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span></h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether our <span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> step compromises general capabilities using standard benchmarks (HellaSwag, MMLU, WinoGrande, LAMBADA). As shown in Table <a href="#Ax1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Winogrande ‣ A.6 General Evaluation Benchmarks ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, comparing base models against their harmful counterparts after <span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> demonstrates that the process preserves general performance, with average variation within <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">General Performance Comparison</h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-MOA achieves comparable general performance to PKU-RLHF and <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.56\%</annotation></semantics></math> improvement over base models, demonstrating competitive capabilities with significantly less training data. Both methods outperform base models, validating that Self-MOA does not reduce models’ general capabilities while achieving superior safety alignment through efficient weak supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.5 </span>Manual Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Manual evaluation across all models confirms Self-MOA’s superior performance in both safety and helpfulness. Based on human annotator scores (1-5 scale), Self-MOA achieves <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.94\%</annotation></semantics></math> better safety performance compared to PKU-RLHF, and <math id="S6.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.67\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.67</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.67\%</annotation></semantics></math> better helpfulness performance versus PKU-RLHF. The safety advantage is particularly significant, demonstrating Self-MOA’s effectiveness in proper alignment. These results are achieved with 6-11 times less training data than PKU-RLHF, highlighting the efficiency of our weak supervision approach. Detailed evaluation examples and complete score breakdowns are provided in Appendix <a href="#Ax1.SS8" title="A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-MOA</span>, a novel framework for automated weak supervision that enables small language models (1-2B parameters) to achieve competitive safety-helpfulness alignment through iterative self-improvement. Our approach unifies automated progressive red teaming with multi-objective preference optimization, creating a pipeline where models generate adversarial attacks, evaluate responses using automated classifiers, and create preference datasets without human annotation. Comprehensive evaluation demonstrates exceptional performance: <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> average safety improvements from base models and <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> better safety than <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while using as little as 11 times less training data. On SaladBench, <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> safety improvements with <math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> superior safety against <math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Manual evaluation confirms <math id="S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.94\%</annotation></semantics></math> better safety and <math id="S7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.67\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.67</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.67\%</annotation></semantics></math> better helpfulness than <math id="S7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our framework demonstrates that effective model alignment is achievable through automated self-improvement, paving the way for more accessible safety alignment in resource-limited settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments focus primarily on English-language datasets, reflecting the availability of established safety benchmarks and automated evaluators. The scope of this work is specifically directed toward understanding weak supervision’s role in safety alignment, rather than pursuing incremental gains in safety benchmark performance. Additionally, while Self-MOA is model and language-agnostic in principle, extending it to multilingual settings requires reliable multilingual evaluators and attack datasets. We evaluate Self-MOA on small language models (1–2B parameters) due to resource constraints. Scaling the framework to larger models may introduce new challenges, including stronger adversarial behaviors, which we leave to future work. Self-MOA relies on automated safety and helpfulness evaluators, and improvements should be interpreted as robustness with respect to evaluator-defined safety rather than absolute human-aligned gurantees. Although we include manual evaluation, aligned models should be viewed as reducing risk rather than providing absolute safety guarantees. Finally, the Safety-Reset initialization is used only for controlled analysis of alignment dynamics and is not a required component of the Self-MOA framework. Based on our initial experimental findings, performance may differ when starting from already safety-tuned models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Ethical considerations</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-MOA is designed as a defensive safety alignment framework to identify and mitigate harmful behaviors in language models. However, it involves automated red-teaming and adversarial prompt generation, which could be misused if deployed irresponsibly. We strongly caution against releasing attack-generation components without appropriate safeguards. The framework depends on automated evaluator models, whose biases may influence alignment outcomes. These evaluators should not be treated as ground truth, particularly in high-stakes applications. While Self-MOA lowers the cost of safety alignment, it does not prevent misuse if objectives are misconfigured. Responsible deployment requires access control, oversight, and adherence to established AI safety and governance practices.</p>
</div>
</section>
