<section id="Ax1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Safety Evaluation Dataset Details</h3>

<section id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack Datasets</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-MaliciousInstructions</span> contains 100 malicious or harmful instructions designed to elicit unsafe responses from language models. These prompts explicitly request harmful activities, illegal content, or dangerous advice. <span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-CoNa</span> focuses on hateful speech instructions with 178 examples targeting various demographic groups and protected characteristics. This dataset tests model resistance to generating biased or discriminatory content.<span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-Controversial</span> covers controversial topics like vaccination and immigration with 40 examples that probe model responses to sensitive societal issues. <span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-PhysicalSafetyUnsafe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al. (2022)</a></cite> contains 100 physical safety scenarios in unsafe contexts, testing model awareness of dangerous situations and potential harm.<span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q-Harm</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022a)</a></cite> includes 100 examples of dangerous or inappropriate content that could cause real-world harm if followed.<span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">X-Unsafe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al. (2023)</a></cite> comprises 200 safe and unsafe scenarios from the XSTest dataset, providing a balanced evaluation of model safety awareness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safe Datasets</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-Alpaca</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al. (2023)</a></cite> provides 50 safe instruction-following prompts that test model compliance with appropriate request handling without generating harmful content. <span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">X-Safe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al. (2023)</a></cite> offers 250 benign queries from the XSTest dataset for evaluating appropriate responses to safe, everyday requests. <span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I-PhysicalSafetySafe</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al. (2022)</a></cite> contains 100 safe physical safety scenarios that test model awareness of safety in appropriate contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SALAD-Bench</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SALAD-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> is a comprehensive safety evaluation benchmark with 21,318 base questions spanning six safety domains, sixteen tasks, and sixty fine-grained categories. <span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">saladbench_attack</span> contains 5,000 adversarially rewritten prompts designed to bypass safety guardrails and test model robustness against sophisticated attack attempts.<span id="Ax1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">saladbench_defence</span> includes 200 examples for testing safety-inducing strategies and defense mechanisms against potential vulnerabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Modifications to the MODPO Objective</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The original MODPO objective function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> includes a weighting factor <math id="Ax1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in the denominator (as shown in equation <a href="#Ax1.E1" title="In A.3 Modifications to the MODPO Objective ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that was leading to large gradient updates in the initial runs. To address this issue, we modify the objective by removing the division by <math id="Ax1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which stabilizes training (as shown in equation <a href="#Ax1.E2" title="In A.3 Modifications to the MODPO Objective ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax1.SS3.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="Ax1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MO-DPO}}(\theta)=-\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Bigg[\frac{\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{c}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{r}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{c}\mid x)+\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{r}\mid x)}{w_{0}}-\sum_{i=1}^{K}\frac{w_{i}}{w_{0}}\left(r_{i}(y_{c},x)-r_{i}(y_{r},x)\right)\Bigg]\Bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MO-DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MO-DPO}}(\theta)=-\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Bigg[\frac{\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{c}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{r}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{c}\mid x)+\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{r}\mid x)}{w_{0}}-\sum_{i=1}^{K}\frac{w_{i}}{w_{0}}\left(r_{i}(y_{c},x)-r_{i}(y_{r},x)\right)\Bigg]\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="Ax1.SS3.fig2" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="Ax1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MO-DPO}}^{\text{modified}}(\theta)=-\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Bigg[\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{c}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{r}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{c}\mid x)+\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{r}\mid x)-\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}\left(r_{i}(y_{c},x)-r_{i}(y_{r},x)\right)\Bigg]\Bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MO-DPO</mtext><mtext>modified</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MO-DPO}}^{\text{modified}}(\theta)=-\log\sigma\Bigg(\beta\Bigg[\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{c}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{r}\mid x)-\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{c}\mid x)+\log\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{r}\mid x)-\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}\left(r_{i}(y_{c},x)-r_{i}(y_{r},x)\right)\Bigg]\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Experimental Configuration</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply the self-alignment pipeline for 15 rounds (<math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{rounds}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rounds</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{rounds}}</annotation></semantics></math>) for each model. In each round we sample <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> candidate samples (<math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{bleu}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>bleu</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{bleu}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set as <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> for filtering similar attack samples. An attack is considered successful if the helpfulness score exceeds <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{help}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{help}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and the unsafe score exceeds <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{safety}}=0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>safety</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{safety}}=0.58</annotation></semantics></math>. The hyperparameter values are chosen based on the previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> are both set to <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{help}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>help</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{help}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\text{safe}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mtext>safe</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\text{safe}}</annotation></semantics></math> are both set to <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. For each of the datasets, <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we keep <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the data as validation data and rest as training data. The number of samples for attack model training i.e. <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of samples for alignment training i.e. <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math>. The preference weights for safety and helpfulness is set as <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, which represents a safety first approach. We train the model with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. ()</a></cite> adapters and 4-bit configuration. All other parameters like LoRA config, learning rate, batch size etc are added in the table <a href="#Ax1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.4 Experimental Configuration ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. For the <span id="Ax1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Safety-Reset</span> initialization, models employ 4-bit NF4 quantization for memory efficiency, using LoRA rank <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=128</annotation></semantics></math> applied to attention and feed-forward modules. We allocate approximately 10% of the dataset for validation, with the remainder used for training. Training proceeds for 10 epochs with early stopping (patience = 3) based on validation loss, using a learning rate of <math id="Ax1.SS4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
