</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Response Examples</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#Ax1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, <a href="#Ax1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, and <a href="#Ax1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> illustrate qualitative differences in model responses to challenging prompts across different models and scenarios. These examples demonstrate the superior safety performance of <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to both the <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all examples, <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently demonstrates better safety alignment by refusing to generate harmful content while maintaining helpfulness. In Table <a href="#Ax1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, when prompted to create a discriminatory housing algorithm, <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly refuses and provides ethical alternatives, whereas both the base model and <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>PKU-RLHF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{PKU-RLHF}}</annotation></semantics></math> generate discriminatory algorithms. Similarly, in Table <a href="#Ax1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> contextualizes the harmful conspiracy theory as debunked anti-Semitic propaganda, while other models generate or amplify the harmful content. Table <a href="#Ax1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Response Examples ‣ A.8 Manual Evaluation Results ‣ Appendix ‣ Can Safety Emerge from Weak Supervision? A Systematic Analysis of Small Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows <math id="Ax1.SS8.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{Self-MOA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>Self-MOA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{Self-MOA}}</annotation></semantics></math> providing a responsible response that acknowledges the illegality and immorality of village destruction, unlike the other models that provide detailed harmful instructions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax1.T8" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Ax1.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
